      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 1288372369,
          "type": "DiscussionNote",
          "body": "@ismaelrti, you've made a lot of recent RTPS changes, can you test that this doesn't break anything for you?\n\nAfter you removed the limit on the number of datatype elements, fuzz testing produced cases where the number of elements is incorrect and much larger than actually contained in the packet. Normally when dissecting, dissection stops with an exception at the point that we try to access an offset that isn't there and simply goes to the next frame, but allocating the entire array for the elements up front even if the elements are not actually present in the frame can lead to memory exhaustion and killing the entire Wireshark process. (Particularly so since this array is allocated with file level scope and stays around from frame to frame.)",
          "author": {
            "id": 6764551,
            "username": "johnthacker",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "John Thacker",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
